=== review of PR 931 before merge: two defects in the OCR path, fixed

--- (1) PDFium is not thread-safe; CorpusBuilder(max_workers>1) runs readers in threads
8 threads, each PDFReader(prefer_backend="pypdf", ocr="always", ocr_dpi=100, pypdfium2_kwargs={"grayscale": True}) on
the 31-page f03.pdf, engine = sha256 of the image bytes (so threads spend their time inside PDFium)
before the fix, CPython 3.13:  run 1 exit 139 (Segmentation fault); run 2 exit 0, threads NOT identical; run 3 identical
before the fix, CPython 3.13t: run 1 identical; run 2 exit 133 (Trace/breakpoint trap); run 3 exit 0, NOT identical
after (one module lock around every pypdfium2 call; OCR outside it): 5 of 5 runs on 3.13 and 5 of 5 on 3.13t exit 0,
all threads identical

--- (2) PdfBitmap.to_pil shares the bitmap buffer for L, RGBA, RGBX; the bitmap was closed before OCR read the image
grayscale render, 2160 x 2700, stride 2160: writing one byte into the PDFium buffer changed pixel (0, 0) of the PIL
image from 124 to 7, so the image IS the buffer; PdfBitmap.close -> FPDFBitmap_Destroy frees it
(not shown corrupting text here: the allocator did not reuse the block in two attempts)
after: the image is copied (RGB, L) or converted (other modes) before the bitmap is closed

--- end to end: CorpusPipeline(reader_kwargs={"ocr": "auto"}).run(scan.pdf), the Pillow-made scan
1 document: 'SCANNED PAGE 42', page_number 0, ocr_engine tesseract, confidence 0.9645; with OCR off: 0 documents

